#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:25
msgid "**New GUI**"
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:27
msgid ""
"The old GUI was relatively difficult to use and quite limited. For example, "
"there was no way to use a custom model outside of the main directory, you’d "
"have to manually put the model files into the main directory of the plugin. "
"There was also no pre- or post-processing, and the resolution of the input "
"image was fixed, which didn’t allow for fine-tuning the result."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:29
msgid "The new GUI looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:31
#, fuzzy
#| msgid ""
#| "![|Screenshot of the plugin in action](images/posts/2024/"
#| "shortcut_composer_screenshot.png)"
msgid ""
"![Screenshot of the FSC plugin GUI](images/posts/2025/"
"fast_sketch_cleanup_feb/FSCPluginScreenshot.png)"
msgstr ""
"![|Ekrankopio de la kromaĵo en ago](images/posts/2024/"
"shortcut_composer_screenshot.png)"

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:33
msgid "**Model**"
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:35
msgid ""
"In this section you can select the model in the File combobox, or you can "
"switch to another folder using either the button with the folder icon (for a "
"custom folder) or the “Reset to default” button (which resets the path to "
"the default path for the plugin). The combobox with models gets updated to "
"show the models from the currently selected folder."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:37
msgid ""
"“Note about the model” presents some notes or hints about usage that were "
"saved into the model information file."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:39
msgid "**Device to use**"
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:41
msgid ""
"Here you can choose whether to use CPU, GPU or NPU. NPU is a new type of "
"device that is only available on some computers, on Windows you should have "
"all the drivers already installed, but if you’re on Linux, you would need to "
"install them manually. CPU is typically the slowest, so if any other is "
"available, use the other one. Unavailable devices should be greyed out."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:43
msgid "**Preview images**"
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:45
msgid ""
"Those are small cutouts of the image on different stages of the processing. "
"First image shows the original sample; the second one shows the result of "
"the pre-processing; third shows the result of the inference (processing "
"through the model) applied to the pre-processed image; and the last one "
"shows the final result."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:47
msgid ""
"Preview size determines how big the preview is. The sample is cut out of the "
"center of the image, with the width and height being equal to the Preview "
"Size \\* Model Input Size (usually 256\\) / Scale. That means that a Preview "
"Size of 8 would update roughly 16x slower than Preview Size of 1, no matter "
"the Scale, and assuming the same model. That might make the dialog less "
"responsive, so be careful with higher values. Sometimes it is useful though "
"to see a bigger preview though."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:49
msgid ""
"If you click on one of the images, it will bring out a dialog showing the "
"same image in a bigger size, and you can click on the buttons or use arrows "
"to navigate to the other images. You can resize that dialog to see even more "
"detail if needed."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:51
msgid "**Pre-processing**"
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:53
msgid ""
"Defines the pre-processing. It’s performed in the order of the widgets in "
"the dialog."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:55
msgid ""
"Levels widget: it’s a quick way to increase contrast in the input image."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:57
msgid ""
"Scale: every model has a specific size of its context window, which means "
"it’s sensitive to resolution. Using Scale you can quickly decrease or "
"increase the resolution of the input image, changing the result in a very "
"significant way. Be careful, it’s a scale for one dimension, meaning that "
"the processing time will increase or decrease exponentially."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:59
msgid "**Post-processing**"
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:61
msgid ""
"Scale: it’s just a widget showing the reversal of the scaling in pre-"
"processing. You can’t change it. It ensures that the result has the same "
"size as the input image."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:63
msgid "Levels widget: it works just like in the pre-processing."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:65
msgid ""
"Sharpen filter: it sharpens the result, with the strength equal to the "
"number from the slider. Zero means input \\= output, every higher value "
"sharpens the result. One means the exact result you’d get from Krita’s "
"normal Sharpen filter."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:67
msgid "**Advanced options**"
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:69
msgid ""
"Invert: usually you don’t need to change this option, because whether it "
"needs to be checked or not is embedded in the model information file (the "
"same one that contains the note). Most models do require this checkbox "
"checked."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:71
msgid "**Run**"
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:73
msgid ""
"Press the button to start processing. It takes the projection (think: “New "
"Layer From Visible”) of the canvas, puts it through all the processing, and "
"then creates a new layer with the result."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:75
msgid ""
"The Run button changes into a Progress Bar to show you progress. When the "
"image is processed, the dialog closes automatically."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:77
msgid "Note that it’s not possible to cancel the processing, unfortunately."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:79
msgid "**Best workflow**"
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:81
msgid ""
"The ultimate best workflow I found to get the best result is to first use "
"SketchyModel.xml with low scale (either 1.0 or often even below that), then "
"either decrease the opacity of the result or put a white semi-opaque layer "
"on top, and then use InkModel.xml. The first model removes unnecessary lines "
"and smoothes the lines out, and the second model creates nice, crisp lines. "
"The only problem with using them one after another is that SketchyModel "
"produces pretty dark lines, while InkModel is sensitive to values and "
"requires the input to be light grey, otherwise it doesn’t work properly, "
"hence the additional white layer."
msgstr ""

#: content/en/posts/2025/fsc-plugin-new-version.md:83
msgid ""
"You can also use InkModel.xml directly, if the sketch is clean enough "
"already."
msgstr ""

